The validation exposed and resolved an ambiguity in the paper’s notation: I will use the literal sets \(C_0=\{z:\alpha^z+1\text{ is a nonzero square}\}\) and \(C_1=\{z:\alpha^z+1\text{ is a nonsquare}\}\), which I checked by enumeration on small primes. The chosen task asks for the sum of two translate intersections. Mechanically this builds both sets and scans them; structurally, their union omits only the exponent of \(-1\), reducing each row to one quadratic-character test. This is Track B, not Track A.
Step 0 supports a native Track B family from Section 3/Theorem 3.2: direct set enumeration is large, while the paper’s cyclotomic partition gives a short exact quadratic-character route. The paper contains no distributional hardness theorem, so Track A would be unjustified.
