Let $(V,\mathcal{B})$ be an incidence structure. Let $p\in V$, and define $\mathcal{B}_{p}=\{\mathcal{B}-\{p\}\mid\mathcal{B}\in\mathcal{B}\text{ and }p\in\mathcal{B}\}$. We call the incidence structure $(V-\{p\},\mathcal{B}_{p})$ the {\it contraction} of $(V,\mathcal{B})$ at $p$. It is clear that contracting at points of a $3$-adesign will give a $2$-adesign as long as not all $3$-sets of points occur in the same number of blocks of the contraction.

\begin{example}\label{ex563} The contraction at the point $p=1$ of the $3$-$(11,5,1)$ adesign in Example \ref{ex562} is a symmetric $2$-$(10,4,1)$ adesign with the ten blocks:
